---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Principle from "../components/Principle.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

import ScaleCostChart from "../components/visuals/ScaleCostChart.astro";
import CitationCard from "../components/visuals/CitationCard.astro";
import OutputDiff from "../components/visuals/OutputDiff.astro";
import AIBadge from "../components/visuals/AIBadge.astro";
import PipelineIO from "../components/visuals/PipelineIO.astro";
import IterationLoop from "../components/visuals/IterationLoop.astro";
import UpstreamComms from "../components/visuals/UpstreamComms.astro";
import BenchmarkComparison from "../components/visuals/BenchmarkComparison.astro";
import FeatureChecklist from "../components/visuals/FeatureChecklist.astro";
import VersionPin from "../components/visuals/VersionPin.astro";
import GovernanceChecklist from "../components/visuals/GovernanceChecklist.astro";
import HotSwapDiagram from "../components/visuals/HotSwapDiagram.astro";
import OpenSourceFlywheel from "../components/visuals/OpenSourceFlywheel.astro";
import BugReportDecisionTree from "../components/visuals/BugReportDecisionTree.astro";
---

<Layout title="rewrites.bio  - A manifesto for bioinformatics">
  <Hero />

  <main class="manifesto">
    <div class="preamble" id="introduction">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="preamble-section">
          <h2 class="preamble-heading reveal">The opportunity</h2>
          <div class="preamble-layout">
            <div class="preamble-text reveal-stagger">
              <p class="reveal">
                Most bioinformatics tools were written for a world that no longer exists -when datasets
                were smaller, compute was slower, and a single lab could run everything on a desktop.
                Today, a typical RNA-seq study processes tens, hundreds, or even thousands of samples. The computational cost
                of slow tools has compounded from a nuisance into a real scientific and environmental
                problem.
              </p>
              <p class="reveal">
                The scale is concrete. A pipeline like nf-core/rnaseq is run by tens of thousands of researchers.
                If one step wastes ten minutes of CPU per sample and the pipeline runs on a million
                samples a year, that is nearly two decades of wasted compute, every year. Wasted
                CPU-hours mean kilowatt-hours and kilograms of CO2. Rewrites that run 10–50× faster
                can save hundreds of tonnes of CO2-equivalent annually.
              </p>
              <p class="reveal">
                What has changed is the cost of solving this. AI coding assistants have shifted the
                equation. A domain expert who understands a tool's inputs, outputs, and edge cases can
                now produce a working reimplementation in days rather than years. Fast code is now
                cheap. Scientific insight, validation, and trust are not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="preamble-visual reveal">
              <ScaleCostChart />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="preamble-section preamble-risk">
          <h2 class="preamble-heading reveal">The risk</h2>
          <div class="preamble-text-full reveal-stagger">
            <p class="reveal">
              Cheap code is not the same as correct code. AI-generated implementations are fluent
              and fast, but they are also confidently wrong in ways that are easy to miss. A rewrite
              that silently produces different results poisons every analysis built on it. A rewrite
              that ignores the original authors' contributions damages the incentive structures that
              produced the tool in the first place.
            </p>
            <p class="reveal">
              Poorly executed rewrites also fragment the community. Three competing rewrites of the
              same tool, each with slightly different behaviour and incomplete documentation, leave
              users unsure which to trust. Abandoned projects litter the landscape, blocking better
              alternatives and confusing newcomers. The existing tools are correct, and that
              correctness is hard-won. Rewrites inherit a responsibility: to match outputs exactly,
              to credit the work they stand on, and to be honest about what they have and have not
              validated.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="preamble-cta">
          <blockquote class="preamble-callout reveal">
            A wave of AI-assisted rewrites is coming.<br />
            The question is not <strong>whether</strong> it will happen,<br />
            but whether it will happen <strong>well</strong>.
          </blockquote>
          <p class="preamble-closing reveal">These are the principles we follow.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Section
      id="philosophy"
      number="I"
      title="Philosophy"
      intro="Before writing any code, three commitments shape everything else. These are not procedural rules. They are a statement of values."
    >
      <Principle id="credit-authors" number="1.1" title="Credit the original authors">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Behind every bioinformatics tool is years of work: algorithm design, edge-case hunting,
            user feedback, and ongoing maintenance, often by people who were underpaid or
            unpaid for it. A rewrite stands entirely on that foundation. Without the original tool's
            existence, test cases, and accumulated community knowledge, a rewrite cannot even be
            built correctly, let alone validated.
          </p>
          <p>
            Credit belongs everywhere: the README, the docs, any paper or preprint, and in
            downstream reports that use results from the rewrite. A dedicated credits page with
            full citations, DOIs, author lists, and version numbers is the minimum. When users
            cite your rewrite, they should know to also cite the original.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is how the scientific community tracks provenance and assigns credit to work that
            enables everything downstream. Obscuring the lineage of a tool undermines the incentive
            structures that motivated the original work.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CitationCard slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="emulate-exactly" number="1.2" title="Emulate exactly">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The goal is not to build a better version of the tool. It is to build a faster version
            that produces the same results. A rewrite that changes outputs, even in ways that look
            like improvements, is a different tool. It cannot inherit the original's validation
            history. It cannot be quietly substituted into existing pipelines without forcing
            users to re-validate their analyses.
          </p>
          <p>
            Emulate exactly means bit-identical or numerically-identical outputs wherever possible.
            For deterministic tools, that means byte-for-byte matching files. For tools with
            floating-point arithmetic, it means results within acceptable numerical precision,
            where "acceptable" is defined by domain scientists, not implementation convenience.
            Header formats, column ordering, file naming, and summary statistics all count.
          </p>
        </div>
        <OutputDiff slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="transparent-ai" number="1.3" title="Be transparent about AI">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            When an AI coding agent writes the implementation, that is a material fact about the
            software. It affects how the code should be trusted, reviewed, and audited. It does
            not mean the software is untrustworthy; correctness is determined by output comparison,
            not by who or what wrote the code. But users and contributors deserve to know how it
            was built.
          </p>
          <p>
            Document clearly in the README and any accompanying paper: which AI tools were used,
            what role they played, and how correctness was verified. "Validated by output
            comparison against mytool v1.0 on a suite of real sequencing datasets" is far more
            useful than leaving provenance implicit. Do not obscure AI involvement to make the
            project seem more traditionally engineered than it is.
          </p>
          <p>
            Output comparison catches what you tested. It does not catch edge cases you have not
            encountered yet. Being transparent also means committing to ongoing validation as new
            datasets and use cases emerge, and being honest about where coverage is thin.
          </p>
        </div>
        <AIBadge slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="planning"
      number="II"
      title="Planning"
      intro="The decisions made before you write the first function determine most of the outcome. Scope, validation strategy, and architecture all matter more than the choice of language."
    >
      <Principle id="think-big" number="2.1" title="Think big">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Some of the biggest performance wins come not just from faster implementations,
            but from rethinking the architecture. Modern bioinformatics
            pipelines chain many discrete tools, each reading from disk, doing work, and writing
            back to disk. Every one of those I/O operations is an opportunity to waste time.
            A straight port to a faster language captures only a fraction of what is available.
          </p>
          <p>
            A single integrated process can avoid materialising intermediate files entirely. Trimming, alignment, and quantification can share data in memory rather than
            writing gigabytes of temporary BAM files between steps. Eliminating redundant I/O is
            often worth more than any algorithmic improvement. Ask: what preprocessing does this
            tool assume has already happened? Can that be folded in? What immediately consumes
            this tool's output? Can that be folded in too?
          </p>
          <p>
            The goal is not to build an unvalidatable monolith. But it is worth thinking beyond
            one-to-one replacement and considering what a pipeline designed from scratch, with what
            we now know about where the bottlenecks are, would actually look like.
          </p>
        </div>
        <PipelineIO slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="work-small" number="2.2" title="Work small">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Thinking big about architecture does not mean building big in one go. The implementation
            strategy should be the opposite: small, tight iteration loops, each step validated
            against the original before moving on. Start with the simplest function that produces
            testable output. Validate it. Then extend.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you implement several components before validating any of them, a discrepancy in
            the final output could come from anywhere. You have created a debugging problem that
            requires mentally decomposing what you just built. Work small, and when something is
            wrong, you already know where to look.
          </p>
          <p>
            AI coding agents are good at generating plausible implementations quickly. They are
            unreliable for correctness, especially in domain-specific numerical behaviour. The
            right response is not to distrust them but to validate continuously. An agent told
            "this step's output doesn't match on paired-end data with adapter sequences" can
            iterate productively. An agent asked to implement an entire pipeline in one prompt
            is much harder to correct.
          </p>
        </div>
        <IterationLoop slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="building"
      number="III"
      title="Building"
      intro="Good intentions don't ship correct software. The building phase is where principles meet reality: data that behaves unexpectedly, performance that doesn't match theory, users who need the tool to work before they will trust it."
    >
      <Principle id="test-and-benchmark" number="3.1" title="Test and benchmark with real data">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Synthetic data is useful for quick iteration but insufficient for validation. Real
            sequencing data has instrument-specific error patterns, adapter contamination, and
            chemistry-dependent quality profiles that generators don't replicate. Test on multiple
            organisms, platforms, and library preps. Test edge cases: empty files, single-read
            files, very large files.
          </p>
          <p>
            Performance claims require the same rigour. "10× faster" is marketing unless it comes
            with reproducible benchmarks: the hardware, the dataset, exact command lines, and
            output comparison. Commit reference inputs and expected outputs to the repository.
            Anyone who clones the repo should be able to reproduce your numbers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Benchmark honestly. If your rewrite is faster on some data types but not others, say
            so. Numbers without context mislead; numbers without reproducibility are anecdotes.
            Every passing test is a commitment -maintain them, and add new ones when users report
            failures.
          </p>
        </div>
        <BenchmarkComparison slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="build-what-you-need" number="3.2" title="Build only what you need">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Feature completeness is not the goal - output correctness on the features that matter
            is. Most tools have accumulated flags and edge cases over years; not all are used in
            your target pipeline. Reimplementing everything increases the validation surface and
            the chance of shipping something broken.
          </p>
          <p>
            Identify exactly which capabilities your pipeline uses. Reimplement those exactly.
            For unsupported features, fail with a clear message pointing to the original tool.
            Never silently ignore flags or produce wrong output for partial implementations.
          </p>
          <p>
            A focused rewrite that does four things correctly is more valuable than one that
            claims fifteen and does twelve right. Scope can expand as the project matures; it
            cannot easily contract once users depend on it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FeatureChecklist slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="pin-versions" number="3.3" title="Pin versions and document">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Every claim your rewrite makes about output equivalence is implicitly a claim about a
            specific version of the original tool. <em>"Compatible with mytool"</em> is meaningless.
            <em>"Produces bit-identical output to mytool v1.0, validated on the dataset described in
            benchmarks/"</em> is a testable assertion. The difference matters when a user hits an
            unexpected discrepancy six months after your release.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pin the exact version of every original tool you have validated against. Document not
            just the version number but the command line used for comparison, the dataset, the
            comparison methodology, and the date. This lives in the repository, not in a blog post
            or your memory, so contributors and users can find it without asking.
          </p>
          <p>
            When the original tool releases a new version, you need a process for deciding whether
            to update your validation target. Major version bumps often change output formats.
            Patch releases often fix bugs that may or may not affect your outputs. Document your
            policy and communicate it to users.
          </p>
        </div>
        <VersionPin slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <Section
      id="stewardship"
      number="IV"
      title="Stewardship"
      intro="A rewrite is not a one-time act. Once other researchers build pipelines on your tool, you have made a commitment. Stewardship means honouring it: maintaining correctness as upstream tools evolve, governing transparently, and giving back to the ecosystem that made the rewrite possible."
    >
      <Principle id="maintain" number="4.1" title="Maintain and govern">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The hard part is not the initial implementation. It is the years of maintenance that
            follow: edge cases users find, re-validation when the original tool updates, new
            sequencing chemistries your test suite doesn't cover, PRs that need reviewing.
            Someone needs to be responsible, and that needs to be visible before you ship.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put governance in place before release: CONTRIBUTING.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, versioned
            tags and changelogs, and a commitment to acknowledge issues. These signal to users
            and contributors that the project takes itself seriously.
          </p>
        </div>
        <GovernanceChecklist slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="preserve-compatibility" number="4.2" title="Preserve compatibility">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The strongest argument for adopting a rewrite is zero migration cost. If your rewrite
            produces identical output files, same names, same formats, same column headers, same
            summary statistics, then swapping it into an existing pipeline is a one-line config
            change. Every MultiQC module that parses the original's output will parse yours.
            Every downstream script that reads the original's results will read yours.
          </p>
          <p>
            This reversibility is what makes adoption risk-free, and risk-free adoption is what
            makes widespread adoption possible. Researchers running production pipelines cannot
            re-validate their entire analysis history every time they update a tool. But they
            will try a rewrite they can test on one run, confirm matches, and roll back if needed.
          </p>
        </div>
        <HotSwapDiagram slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="open-source" number="4.3" title="Release as open source">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            The tools you are rewriting were built by the open-source community, validated
            against publicly funded data, and distributed freely. A rewrite that takes this
            foundation and closes the source extracts value without contributing back. Open
            source is also better for adoption: researchers need to inspect code to understand
            what it does to their data, run it on HPC clusters where commercial software is
            often unavailable, and fork it if the project is abandoned.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check licenses before you start. Permissive licenses (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD) let
            you release under any license; copyleft (GPL) requires compatible licensing.
            Whether a reimplementation is a "derived work" depends on jurisdiction - err on
            the side of caution. Choose MIT, Apache-2.0, or BSD unless the original requires
            otherwise. License archaeology is far easier before you ship than after.
          </p>
        </div>
        <OpenSourceFlywheel slot="visual" />
      </Principle>

      <Principle id="contribute-upstream" number="4.4" title="Contribute upstream responsibly">
        <div slot="description">
          <p>
            Building a rewrite puts you in an unusual position: you have studied the original
            tool closely, tested it on diverse data, and found its edge cases. If you find a
            genuine bug you can reproduce in the original tool with a minimal, clean test case,
            report it. That is a real contribution to the scientific community.
          </p>
          <p>
            But be careful before filing bug reports. AI coding agents are fluent and confident,
            and they are wrong in subtle ways that are easy to miss. An agent may convince you
            the original tool is handling a particular input incorrectly when what is actually
            happening is that your reimplementation is wrong, or the input is malformed, or you
            are misreading the spec. Before reporting upstream, verify manually with the original
            tool on clean, well-understood data, not with an AI-generated test case.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not automate bug reports. Open-source maintainers are often volunteers. A
            firehose of machine-generated issues, even polite ones, burns out people whose
            continued work benefits everyone. The bar for filing upstream should be: you are
            confident this is a real bug, you can reproduce it cleanly, and you have considered
            whether you can also contribute a fix.
          </p>
        </div>
        <BugReportDecisionTree slot="visual" />
      </Principle>
    </Section>

    <div class="closing">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="closing-line"></div>
        <p class="closing-text">These principles are a starting point, not a checklist.</p>
        <p class="closing-body">
          Every rewrite is different. Some principles will be easier to honour than others in
          your context. What matters is that the intent behind each one shapes the decisions you
          make when the easy path and the right path diverge. The wave is coming. Let's make
          sure it lifts all boats.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-attribution">
          Discuss and contribute at <a href="https://github.com/seqeralabs/rewrites.bio">github.com/seqeralabs/rewrites.bio</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .manifesto {
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  /* ── Preamble ── */
  .preamble {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    scroll-margin-top: 72px;
  }

  .preamble-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .preamble-visual {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(52px + var(--space-lg));
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .preamble-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .preamble-visual {
      position: static;
    }
  }

  .preamble-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .preamble-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--rust-400);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .preamble p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .preamble-text-full {
    max-width: 800px;
  }

  .preamble-risk {
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
  }

  .preamble-cta {
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .preamble-callout {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--rust-400);
    border-left: none;
    padding-left: 0;
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .preamble-callout strong {
    color: var(--rust-300);
  }

  .preamble-closing {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }

  /* ── Closing ── */
  .closing {
    padding: var(--space-2xl) 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .closing-line {
    width: 48px;
    height: 2px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--rust-600), var(--rust-400));
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-xl);
  }

  .closing-text {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 1.6rem);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .closing-body {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg);
  }

  .closing-attribution {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
  }

  .closing-attribution a {
    color: var(--rust-400);
  }
</style>
